PROPAGATION MODEL

HF Predict runs VOACAP, the Voice of America Coverage Analysis Program, as
its propagation engine. VOACAP was developed for the Voice of America from
the 1983 version of IONCAP, the HF model of the Institute for
Telecommunication Sciences (NTIA/ITS), with work by the Naval Research
Laboratory. Its theory is due to John Lloyd, George Haydon, Donald Lucas and
Larry Teters; George Lane steered its development at the Voice of America;
Franklin Rhoads of NRL made major improvements; Greg Hand of NTIA/ITS designed
many of its later features and maintained it.

The engine is built from voacapl, the gfortran port of VOACAP by James
(Jim) Watson, HZ1JW / M0DNS, https://github.com/jawatson/voacapl, without its
two GPL utilities. The port's changes are released under Creative Commons
CC0. The full notice from voacapl is in engines/voacapl/LICENSE-voacapl.txt
and is installed with the engine as LICENSE-voacapl.txt. It reproduces the
following notice from the VOACAP distribution:

